1) Introduction:
BFD is a modular shell script for parsing application logs and checking for
authentication failures. It ships with 30 service rules covering SSH, mail,
FTP, web, database, and VoIP services. Each rule declares fail2ban-compatible
<HOST> regex patterns; the engine handles log reading, IP extraction, IPv6
normalization, and validation.

BFD uses a log tracking system so logs are only parsed from the point which
they were last read. This greatly assists in performance as we are not
constantly reading the same log data. The log tracking system is compatible
with syslog/logrotate style log rotations which allows it to detect when
rotations have happened and grab log tails from both the new log file and
the rotated log file.

Failure counting uses a sliding time window (default 5 minutes) — only
recent failures count toward the trigger threshold, and old events expire
automatically. Bans can be temporary with auto-expiry, and repeat offenders
are escalated to permanent bans. Both IPv4 and IPv6 addresses are fully
supported.

You can leverage BFD to block attackers using any number of tools such as
APF, iptables, firewalld, nftables, ip route, or execute any custom command.
There is also a fully customizable e-mail alerting system with an e-mail
template that is well suited for every day use.

2) Supported Distributions:
BFD has been tested and is supported on the following distributions:

  RHEL-family:
    - CentOS 6, 7
    - Rocky Linux 8, 9, 10

  Debian-family:
    - Ubuntu 14.04, 16.04, 18.04, 20.04, 22.04, 24.04
    - Debian 12

Log paths are auto-detected at runtime:
  - RHEL: /var/log/secure, /var/log/messages, /var/log/maillog
  - Debian/Ubuntu: /var/log/auth.log, /var/log/syslog, /var/log/mail.log

3) Installation:
The included install.sh script will install BFD to '/usr/local/bfd' and
place a 3-minute cronjob in '/etc/cron.d/bfd'. The setup script will also
execute an included 'importconf' script if you have a previous version of
BFD installed, which will import your previous settings.

On systems with systemd, a systemd timer is also installed (but not enabled
by default). To use it instead of cron:
  systemctl enable --now bfd.timer

Requirements: must be run as root.
  ./install.sh

4) Configuration:
The configuration file is located at '/usr/local/bfd/conf.bfd'. Key options:

  Detection:
  TRIG="15"              - failed logins before an address is blocked
                           (override per-rule in /usr/local/bfd/rules/)
  TRIG_WINDOW="300"      - sliding window in seconds (default 5 min);
                           only failures within this window count
  TRIG_GLOBAL="0"        - cross-service aggregate threshold (0=disabled);
                           when set, failures from ALL services count

  Banning:
  BAN_COMMAND="..."      - command to execute when banning a host
  BAN_DURATION="300"     - ban duration in seconds (0=permanent)
  UNBAN_COMMAND=""       - command to remove a ban (required for auto-unban)

  Repeat offender escalation:
  BAN_PERMANENT_AFTER="5"   - temp bans before permanent (0=disabled)
  BAN_PERMANENT_WINDOW="86400" - window for counting repeats (default 24h)

  IPv6 (optional, leave empty to use BAN_COMMAND for both):
  BAN_COMMAND_V6=""      - IPv6-specific ban command (ip6tables, nft, etc.)
  UNBAN_COMMAND_V6=""    - IPv6-specific unban command

  Alerts:
  EMAIL_ALERTS="0"       - send email alerts [0=off, 1=on]
  EMAIL_ADDRESS="root"   - alert recipient(s), comma-separated

5) Firewall Integration:
Configure BAN_COMMAND in conf.bfd for your firewall:

  APF (default):
    BAN_COMMAND="/etc/apf/apf -d $ATTACK_HOST {bfd.$MOD}"

  iptables (CentOS 6/7, Ubuntu 14-20):
    BAN_COMMAND="/sbin/iptables -I INPUT -s $ATTACK_HOST -j DROP"

  firewalld (Rocky 8+, CentOS 7):
    BAN_COMMAND="/usr/bin/firewall-cmd --permanent --add-rich-rule='rule family=ipv4 source address=$ATTACK_HOST drop'"

  nftables (Rocky 9+, Debian 12, Ubuntu 22+):
    BAN_COMMAND="/usr/sbin/nft add rule inet filter input ip saddr $ATTACK_HOST drop"

  ip route null-route (all distros):
    BAN_COMMAND="/sbin/ip route add blackhole $ATTACK_HOST/32"

  Port-specific blocking (uses $PORTS from rule files):
    BAN_COMMAND="/sbin/iptables -I INPUT -s $ATTACK_HOST -p tcp -m multiport --dports $PORTS -j DROP"

  For temporary bans, also set UNBAN_COMMAND to the reverse operation:
    UNBAN_COMMAND="/sbin/iptables -D INPUT -s $ATTACK_HOST -j DROP"

  For IPv6, set BAN_COMMAND_V6 (or leave empty to use BAN_COMMAND for both):
    BAN_COMMAND_V6="/sbin/ip6tables -I INPUT -s $ATTACK_HOST -j DROP"
    UNBAN_COMMAND_V6="/sbin/ip6tables -D INPUT -s $ATTACK_HOST -j DROP"

6) Usage:
  bfd -s               run with output
  bfd -q               run quietly (cron mode)
  bfd -d               dry-run: detect but do not ban
  bfd -a               show attack pool (top attackers, per-service
                       breakdown, and ban status for each IP)
  bfd -a [STRING]      search attack pool for a string
  bfd -c               health check: validate config, log paths, rules,
                       and state without banning anything
  bfd -l               list active bans
  bfd -u IP            unban an IP address
  bfd -b IP [SERVICE]  manually ban an IP address (permanent)
  bfd -v               display version
  bfd -h               display help

The dry-run mode (-d) runs full detection but logs "would ban" instead
of executing the ban command. Use this to test rules safely.

The health check mode (-c) validates your configuration, checks that log
files and firewall binaries exist, reports which rules are active vs
inactive, and shows the number of active bans. Use this to diagnose
issues without affecting production.

7) Rule Customization:
Rules are located under '/usr/local/bfd/rules/'. Each rule auto-enables
based on the existence of a specific application binary (REQ). For example,
if '/usr/sbin/sshd' exists, the sshd rule is active.

BFD ships with 30 rules:
  SSH:   sshd, dropbear
  Mail:  dovecot, courier, postfix, sendmail, exim_authfail, exim_nxuser,
         vpopmail, cyrus-imap
  FTP:   vsftpd, vsftpd2, proftpd, pure-ftpd
  Web:   apache-auth, nginx-http-auth, modsec, wordpress, roundcube
  Panel: cpanel, plesk, webmin, directadmin
  DB:    mysqld-auth
  VPN:   openvpnas
  VoIP:  asterisk_badauth, asterisk_iax, asterisk_nopeer
  Legacy: rh_imapd, rh_ipop3d

Each rule has a per-service trigger threshold (e.g., sshd=5, dovecot=10).
To customize, edit the rule file and set:
  TRIG="10"

Rules define PORTS for port-specific blocking (e.g., PORTS="22" for sshd).
To disable alerts for a specific rule, uncomment:
  SKIP_ALERT="1"

Use 'bfd -c' to see which rules are active on your system.

8) Ignore Lists:
  /usr/local/bfd/ignore.hosts     - IPs to never ban (one per line)
  /usr/local/bfd/exclude.files    - additional files containing IPs to ignore

BFD automatically detects local IPv4 and IPv6 addresses and excludes them
from bans.

9) Ban Management:
Bans can be temporary (auto-expire after BAN_DURATION seconds) or permanent.
Temporary bans require UNBAN_COMMAND to be set for the firewall rule to be
removed automatically. Repeat offenders are escalated to permanent bans
after BAN_PERMANENT_AFTER temporary bans within BAN_PERMANENT_WINDOW.

  bfd -l               list all active bans with service, ports, and expiry
  bfd -u 10.0.0.1      unban an IP address
  bfd -b 10.0.0.1      manually ban an IP permanently
  bfd -b 10.0.0.1 sshd manually ban with a service label

Ban state is stored in /usr/local/bfd/tmp/:
  bans.active   - currently active bans (timestamp, expiry, IP, service, ports)
  bans.history  - append-only log of all ban/unban events

10) IPv6 Support:
BFD detects and bans both IPv4 and IPv6 addresses automatically. Rules do
not need modification — the extraction engine handles both address families.

For IPv6-specific firewall commands (e.g., ip6tables), set BAN_COMMAND_V6
and UNBAN_COMMAND_V6 in conf.bfd. When empty, BAN_COMMAND is used for both
address families — this works with tools that handle both protocols (nft
with inet family, APF, ip route).

Local IPv6 addresses (including ::1) are auto-detected and excluded from
bans.

11) Troubleshooting:
  - Run 'bfd -c' for a full health check — validates config, log paths,
    firewall binaries, rule status, state directories, and active bans.
  - "locked subsystem, already running?" — a previous BFD run is still
    active or was killed. The lock auto-clears after 300 seconds.
  - "BAN_COMMAND binary not found" — the configured firewall tool is
    not installed. Update BAN_COMMAND in conf.bfd.
  - Rules not triggering — check that the log path exists and the
    application is writing to the expected log file. Use 'bfd -d' to
    test detection without banning. Use 'bfd -c' to see which rules
    are active.
  - On Debian/Ubuntu, ensure log paths are auto-detected correctly.
    Override in conf.bfd if needed: AUTH_LOG_PATH, MAIL_LOG_PATH.
  - "UNBAN_COMMAND is empty" — set UNBAN_COMMAND in conf.bfd for
    temporary bans to auto-remove firewall rules on expiry.

